              "text": "A community garden can produce more than vegetables, but its success depends on arrangements that are often overlooked. Before planting begins, members need to decide how shared tasks will be (1) ______. If everyone assumes that another person will water the seedlings, even a carefully designed garden can fail. Clear responsibilities make cooperation less dependent on chance.\n\nBefore any planting can begin, access to water is another (2) ______ concern. A site may receive plenty of sunlight yet have no convenient tap. Carrying heavy containers from a distant building can quickly discourage volunteers. Planning must therefore take local conditions into (3) ______, rather than treating every empty space as equally suitable for cultivation.\n\nRules about harvesting also require discussion. Some groups divide the ground into separate plots, while others (4) ______ their harvest and distribute it among members. Neither arrangement automatically guarantees fairness. What matters is that participants understand the rules before investing their time and can raise concerns when the rules no longer fit their needs.\n\nA written record provides useful (5) ______ when experienced organizers leave. It should explain routine tasks and identify the person responsible for equipment. Such information is particularly valuable to newcomers, who may otherwise remain (6) ______ to contribute because they do not know where to begin.\n\nThe garden should also remain open to (7) ______. A schedule that works during a holiday may become unsuitable once classes resume. Members can meet every two weeks, returning (8) ______ to compare the plan with actual participation and make adjustments. In this way, a small difficulty can be addressed before it develops into a lasting (9) ______. The aim is not to remove every disagreement, but to establish a (10) ______ basis for sharing both the effort and the harvest.",
