              "text": "Instructions for repairing household objects often assume more knowledge than their readers possess. A writer may describe a step as simple while leaving out the action that makes it possible. For a beginner, the missing detail can become an (1) ______ to completing the task. The challenge is to explain a procedure without either overwhelming the reader or taking essential knowledge for granted.\n\nOne useful approach is to (2) ______ a person attempting the task for the first time. Instead of immediately correcting every mistake, the writer can note where the reader pauses or asks a question. These moments reveal a (3) ______ between what the instructions say and what the reader needs to know.\n\nPictures should be selected with the same care. An attractive photograph of the finished object may provide little (4) ______ about how to reach that result. A closer view showing the position of a small component can be more useful, especially when words alone might leave its orientation (5) ______.\n\nThe order of information matters as well. A warning that appears after the relevant action cannot serve its purpose. Essential precautions should come (6) ______ the step to which they apply. Optional background information, by contrast, can be placed separately so that it does not (7) ______ the sequence of actions.\n\nFinally, the instructions should be tested after they have been revised. Familiarity can make a writer (8) ______ omissions that are obvious to someone else. A fresh reader offers an independent perspective and may uncover a problem that the earlier revision did not (9) ______. Good instructions are therefore the product of repeated attention to actual use, not merely of (10) ______ in the writer's own expertise.",
